              "text": "Men have larger and more prominent Adam\u2019s apples primarily due to **hormonal differences during puberty**, which cause the voice box and surrounding cartilage to grow larger and shift anatomically. \n\nTo understand exactly why this happens, it helps to look at the anatomy, the role of hormones, and the physics of the human voice.\n\n---\n\n### 1. What actually is an \"Adam's apple\"?\nThe Adam\u2019s apple is not a standalone bone or organ; it is a protrusion of the **thyroid cartilage**. This is a tough, shield-like structure made of cartilage that wraps around and protects your larynx (voice box). \n\n### 2. The Role of Testosterone during Puberty\nBefore puberty, boys and girls have similarly sized larynxes and thyroid cartilages. However, when puberty hits:\n* Boys experience a massive surge in **testosterone**.\n* Testosterone acts as a growth signal, causing the larynx to grow significantly larger and heavier.\n* To accommodate and protect this much larger voice box, the surrounding thyroid cartilage also expands.\n\n### 3. The Angle of the Cartilage\nThe thyroid cartilage is made of two flat plates (laminae) that join together at the front of the neck, like the bow of a ship. The angle at which these plates meet is what determines how much the \"bump\" sticks out:\n* **In females:** The plates meet at a wider, more obtuse angle of about **120 degrees**. This creates a rounded, flatter shape that blends smoothly into the neck.\n* **In males:** Because the larynx grows so much larger, the plates meet at a sharper, more acute angle of about **90 degrees** (and sometimes even sharper). This sharp angle forces the cartilage to protrude forward, creating the distinct bump we call the Adam's apple.\n\n### 4. The Functional Purpose: A Deeper Voice\nThe physical growth of the larynx is directly tied to the pitch of the voice. \nThink of the vocal cords inside the larynx like the strings on a guitar or piano:\n* Short, thin strings vibrate quickly and produce a high-pitched sound.\n* Long, thick strings vibrate slowly and produce a deep, low-pitched sound.\n\nBecause men have a larger larynx, they have **longer and thicker vocal cords**. When air passes over these longer vocal cords, they vibrate at a lower frequency, which gives men their characteristically deeper, lower-pitched voices.\n\n### Do women have Adam's apples?\nYes, **everyone has one**. Women have a larynx and a thyroid cartilage protecting it, just as men do. However, because women do not undergo the testosterone-driven laryngeal growth spurt during puberty, their thyroid cartilage remains smaller and flatter. Additionally, women typically have a higher percentage of subcutaneous body fat around the neck, which further cushions and hides the structure.",
